# `tools` is deliberately absent from Permissions-Policy below: a feature no
# header names keeps its default allowlist, and WebMCP's is already 'self'.
# Naming it would change nothing here while putting the camera, geolocation and
# microphone denials behind one more chance to mistype a structured field — a
# Permissions-Policy that fails to parse is dropped whole, denials included.
#
# Origin-Agent-Cluster is not hardening. WebMCP rejects registerTool, getTools
# and executeTool with SecurityError on any non-file scheme whose agent cluster
# is not origin-keyed, and site-keyed is the default a site does not choose.
#
# Origin-Trial token below: for https://rapier.website (isSubdomain true), feature WebMCP,
# expires 2026-11-17 00:00 UTC; the origin trial spans Chrome 149-156, shipping unflagged at 157.
# The same token rides in the head of rapier.html as a meta tag; the static audit pins the two
# equal, so a renewal edits both.
#
# Content-Security-Policy: the browser-owned half of the content boundary. The sanitizer refuses
# a hostile document's request; this refuses the request that gets past the sanitizer, and it is
# the only owner that cannot be reasoned around. `default-src 'none'` means every fetch this
# page does not name below is refused, so a directive absent here is a capability absent.
#   script-src and style-src name no 'self': every byte this page executes and every rule it
#     applies is inline, so a same-origin source would admit a file that does not exist.
#     'unsafe-inline' is the application itself, the vendors at rest included — they execute by
#     appending script elements carrying the text they inflated, which no nonce can reach. The
#     two are named in one breath and do not fail alike, measured: without script's the page
#     defines no library and renders nothing, while without style's it works and is unstyled.
#     'unsafe-eval' is absent, and its absence is the point: it was here for one thing, the
#     framework evaluator that compiled each markup directive with the Function constructor.
#     The chrome is render functions now and the markup carries no directives, so nothing in
#     this page builds code from a string. Restoring the allowance would mean something started
#     to.
#   worker-src 'self' blob: is the parse and structure Workers, both built from object URLs,
#     plus the service worker a hosted install registers from this origin. Without blob:
#     WYSIWYG never renders; without 'self' the install's own worker is refused.
#   connect-src 'self' is the three fetch call sites, all same-origin: the optional Math and
#     DOCX bundles, the AGPL source offer re-reading this page, and ?demo=1.
#   img-src and media-src name https: because CONSENT IS A SURFACE. `Load remote content` is a
#     person deciding that this document may reach the place it named, and a header cannot see
#     a tap: strict img-src leaves the tap swapping the placeholder for a picture the browser
#     then refuses, which is an affordance that lies. The pre-consent owner is the sanitizer,
#     which asks the value of every attribute rather than a list of names, so this is not the
#     directive standing between a hostile document and a stranger. data: is the inline raster
#     the sanitizer admits; blob: is the image decoder loading a page-minted body.
#   font-src data: is the two embedded subsets. Consent does not reach here: after a tap an
#     off-origin @font-face or @import still does not load, because a stylesheet and a font are
#     a larger capability than the picture the person was looking at.
#   manifest-src 'self' is the PWA identity. It is the one directive below that no automated
#     surface drives: a browser fetches the manifest during an installability check, which a
#     headless run never performs, so it stands on the install path rather than on a witness.
#   frame-ancestors is unrestricted because being framed is a product surface: ?embed=1 is
#     authorised by an exact parent origin through a MessagePort handshake in the page, and a
#     header restricting ancestors would silently delete the host-owned embed instead.
#   base-uri and form-action are 'none' because this page has no base element and submits
#     nowhere; both are the header-level owner of a document that tried to add one.
